---
/**
 * Biome motion primitives — the two of the six in docs/BIOMES.md that
 * global.css does not have yet: `.boot` and `.focus`.
 *
 * ── PAGE-SCOPED — PENDING CONSOLIDATION INTO global.css ──────────────────────
 * The other four primitives are already shipped and are REUSED by the biome
 * pages, never re-implemented here:
 *   .draw  → global.css `.draw-path` (+ the `.fx-io .draw-path.in` Firefox path)
 *   .count → global.css `.count` / `.count-real` + the `@property --n` counter
 *   .rise  → global.css `.rise` (view-timeline path) and `.beat` (IO path)
 *   .drift → global.css `drift-a` through `--atmo-motion` on <body>, and
 *            `.plate-drift` for a photo plate
 * This file defines no seventh primitive.
 *
 * `is:global` is deliberate. The three pages write these class names on their
 * OWN elements, which a component-scoped selector cannot reach. The class names
 * and the custom properties below are exactly the ones docs/BIOMES.md publishes,
 * so this block lifts into global.css verbatim, with no token value touched.
 *
 * ── THE LCP CONTRACT, read before changing anything here ─────────────────────
 * Nothing in this file applies `opacity: 0`, `clip-path`, or a start mask to
 * anything. Specifically:
 *   - `.boot-scan` is a decorative 1px line that rests one frame-height OUTSIDE
 *     its own `overflow: hidden` frame. It is the animation, not content: it is
 *     `pointer-events: none`, carries `aria-hidden` at every call site, and can
 *     never be an LCP candidate. `.boot-frame` is only ever put around blocks
 *     that contain no focusable element, so the clip cannot swallow an outline.
 *   - `.boot-lock` is a 4px translate on a readout that is legible on every
 *     frame of the animation. No opacity, no clip.
 *   - `.boot-wake` bottoms out at `opacity: 0.32`, never 0.
 *   - `.focus` is a static grade, not an entry animation.
 * The hero, the h1 and the lead paragraph are not touched by any of it.
 *
 * ── WHY THREE PAGES CAN SHARE ONE PRIMITIVE AND STILL MOVE DIFFERENTLY ───────
 * The rhythm is a per-page parameter, not a per-page primitive. Pages set
 * `--boot-ease` / `--boot-dur` on their own `.boot-frame`:
 *   blueprint  linear                    — a drafting-table sweep, even and slow
 *   red        cubic-bezier(.55,.06,.68,.19) — ease-in, it accelerates at you
 *   amber      steps(12, end)            — 12 discrete jumps, mechanical
 * That is the whole difference between the three boot sequences.
 */
---
<style is:global>
  /* ═══════════ .boot — "system online" ═══════════ */

  /* The frame the sweep travels inside. The clip is what lets the sweep END
     outside the visible box, which is why the primitive needs no `opacity: 0`
     and no mask anywhere. Contents must be non-focusable (see the note above). */
  .boot-frame {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .boot-scan {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* ONE 1px line at the top of the element — the `0 0 / 100% 1px` layer — and
       the gradient fades its own ends, so the primitive needs no mask-image.
       Reusing the facility ramp, not the accent: a sweep is not a signal. */
    background: linear-gradient(
        90deg,
        transparent,
        var(--color-facility-300) 18%,
        var(--color-facility-100) 50%,
        var(--color-facility-300) 82%,
        transparent
      )
      no-repeat 0 0 / 100% 1px;
    transform: translateY(-101%);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .boot-scan {
      animation: boot-scan var(--boot-dur, 420ms) var(--boot-ease, linear)
        var(--boot-delay, 80ms) both;
    }
    @keyframes boot-scan {
      from {
        transform: translateY(-101%);
      }
      to {
        transform: translateY(101%);
      }
    }

    /* The page's first readout "locking in". A 4px settle, transform only, so
       the value is legible on every frame. `steps(1, end)` reads as a latch:
       hold, then snap. Keep this OFF anything that already owns an `animation`
       (notably `.count`) — a second `animation` shorthand would cancel it. */
    .boot-lock {
      animation: boot-lock var(--boot-dur, 420ms)
        steps(var(--boot-lock-steps, 1), end) var(--boot-delay, 80ms) both;
    }
    @keyframes boot-lock {
      from {
        transform: translateY(4px);
      }
      to {
        transform: none;
      }
    }

    /* The console wake. A one-shot flicker on a stepped timing function: each
       keyframe interval holds then snaps, so it reads as a failing strip light
       finding its circuit rather than as a fade. Diegetic — this is the same
       vocabulary as `.flicker` and `.dot`, which global.css reserves for the
       hero title and live telemetry; this is the console's version of it.
       Floors at 0.32: the panel is never fully dark, so it never loses its
       visible area and can sit anywhere on the page. */
    .boot-wake {
      animation: boot-wake var(--boot-wake-dur, 900ms) steps(1, end)
        var(--boot-delay, 120ms) both;
    }
    @keyframes boot-wake {
      0% {
        opacity: 0.32;
      }
      7% {
        opacity: 0.9;
      }
      11% {
        opacity: 0.2;
      }
      16% {
        opacity: 1;
      }
      21% {
        opacity: 0.5;
      }
      27% {
        opacity: 1;
      }
      100% {
        opacity: 1;
      }
    }
  }

  /* ═══════════ .focus — the camera finding the subject ═══════════ */

  /* Applied to a WRAPPER, never to the <img>. Two reasons, both structural:
     the grade then COMPOSES with the `.shot-link:hover img` brightness the site
     already ships instead of fighting it in the cascade, and the wrapper is a
     plain <div>/<figure> whose descendants are all position: relative — so the
     containing block a `filter` creates cannot strand anything. (GuideLayout
     renders <Lightbox /> as a SIBLING of the page slot, so no <dialog> is ever
     inside a `.focus` node.)
     `filter` is NOT compositor-only → capped at 4 nodes per page, below the fold.
     The graded state is the DEFAULT, so no-JS, no-hover and reduced-motion all
     get the finished look and hover clears it. It is never animated on entry: a
     filter animation would repaint a large image on every frame. */
  .focus {
    filter: saturate(0.84) contrast(1.05) brightness(0.95);
    transition: filter 0.4s var(--ease-terminal);
  }

  @media (hover: hover) {
    .focus:hover {
      filter: none;
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .focus {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  /* ═══════════ Reduced motion: every animation in this file, named ═══════════
     `.boot-scan` returns to its authored rest position (one frame-height above
     the clip, i.e. out of the box) — the correct static answer for a sweep, and
     nothing a reader needs is hidden. `.boot-lock` returns to `transform: none`,
     so the readout simply sits where it belongs. `.boot-wake` returns to
     `opacity: 1`, the fully-lit console. Every one of the three is a complete,
     finished state; none is left half-applied. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .boot-scan,
    .boot-lock,
    .boot-wake {
      animation: none;
    }
    .boot-lock {
      transform: none;
    }
    .boot-wake {
      opacity: 1;
    }
  }
</style>
